INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - EVENING

A big square table, worn pale by decades of elbows. A pot of soup on the stove, forgotten. A rotary phone on the wall. The window shows a field gone blue with dusk.

CORDELIA ASHWORTH, 58, the elder sister, ironing a tablecloth with slow, deliberate strokes, watches her younger sister MAEVE ASHWORTH, 54, who sits at the far end of the table with a suitcase already packed beside her chair, her coat folded neatly across her lap.

The iron hisses. Cordelia reaches the corner of the cloth, smooths it, folds it once, and begins another pass.

CORDELIA
You don't have to go tonight.

MAEVE
The bus leaves at nine.

CORDELIA
There's a bus at six tomorrow.

MAEVE
I'd rather not wait.

Cordelia sets the iron down on its heel. Crosses to the stove. Lifts the lid on the soup and stirs, not tasting.

CORDELIA
Mother's will is in the drawer. You haven't looked at it.

MAEVE
I don't need to.

CORDELIA
It says the house is ours.

MAEVE
It says the house is yours. I've read it.

CORDELIA
You said you hadn't.

MAEVE
I lied.

Cordelia puts the spoon down so softly it doesn't clink. She wipes her hands on her apron, one finger at a time, then turns, and for a long moment looks at her sister's face, the tight jaw, the rim of red around the eyes that Maeve will not let fall. She was always the stubborn one, and she is stubborn now, and she is leaving the way she did at eighteen, with her coat on her lap and her chin high, and Cordelia knows that if she says what she wants to say she will shatter the entire table, so she picks up the iron again.

CORDELIA (CONT'D)
Then go.

MAEVE
That's it?

CORDELIA
What did you want?

MAEVE
I wanted you to say stay.

CORDELIA
I did. Twice.

MAEVE
You said I didn't have to go.

CORDELIA
That's the same thing.

MAEVE
It's not. It's never been the same thing.

She stands. Lifts the suitcase. Holds it with both hands, like something borne from a flood.

MAEVE (CONT'D)
Goodbye, Cordie.

The nickname lands. Cordelia's hand tightens on the iron. A thin trail of steam rises, curling past her face. Behind her the soup begins to boil over, hissing on the burner. She doesn't turn.

CORDELIA
Don't let the door slam.

Maeve closes the door gently behind her. The latch clicks.

Cordelia lets the iron fall onto the tablecloth. The smell of scorched cotton rises. She stands there, watching it burn.
